# Coco Tree Service — Design System

## Design Direction

The visual identity must communicate:

- Nature
- Trust
- Professionalism
- Safety
- Speed
- Technical capability
- Premium service

The visual language should feel cinematic, natural, modern, and professional.

Avoid generic landscaping or construction aesthetics.

The design should combine natural environments with subtle visual references to professional tree-service equipment and operations.

## Color Philosophy

The primary visual environment should be based on dark natural tones.

Preferred palette:

- Deep Forest: #0B1F16
- Forest Green: #173D2B
- Natural Green: #2F6B45
- Earth: #5A4634
- Warm Sand: #D8C7A3
- Off White: #F3F1EA
- Pure White: #FFFFFF
- Charcoal: #111412

Use the palette consistently.

Dark forest tones should dominate large visual areas.

Warm sand and off-white should be used for typography and contrast.

Natural green should be used for accents and interactive states.

Earth tones may be used to reinforce the natural environment.

Do not introduce unrelated bright colors.

## Contrast

Text must remain readable against imagery and backgrounds.

When text overlays photography or video:

- use appropriate overlays
- use gradients when necessary
- maintain sufficient contrast
- avoid placing important text over visually noisy areas

Do not sacrifice readability for visual effects.

## Typography

Typography should communicate:

- authority
- clarity
- modern professionalism
- premium quality

Use a strong display typeface for major headlines and a highly readable sans-serif for body text.

Typography hierarchy should include:

- Display
- H1
- H2
- H3
- Body Large
- Body
- Small
- Caption

Avoid excessive font sizes on mobile.

Do not introduce additional font families without a design reason.

## Spacing

Use a consistent spacing system.

Prefer existing spacing tokens over arbitrary values.

Large spacing should be used between major landing page sections.

Tighter spacing should be used inside cards and UI components.

The design should feel spacious rather than crowded.

## Layout

The site should use:

- wide cinematic compositions
- strong visual hierarchy
- generous whitespace
- controlled content widths
- large imagery
- asymmetric compositions where appropriate

Avoid excessive use of centered text and symmetrical card grids.

The visual rhythm should vary between sections.

## Border Radius

Use restrained corner rounding.

Large rounded cards should not dominate the visual identity.

Prefer moderate radii for:

- cards
- buttons
- media containers
- forms

Maintain consistency throughout the interface.

## Buttons

Primary CTA buttons should be visually prominent but refined.

Primary CTA:

- strong contrast
- clear label
- comfortable touch area
- subtle hover animation
- clear focus state

Secondary buttons should have lower visual emphasis.

Avoid excessive button variations.

Recommended CTA language should communicate action clearly, for example:

- Get a Free Estimate
- Request a Quote
- Schedule Service
- Contact Us

Do not use vague CTA labels such as:

- Learn More
- Click Here

unless context requires them.

## Cards

Cards should not look like generic dashboard components.

For service cards:

- prioritize real photography
- use large visual areas
- support hover interaction on desktop
- support expansion where appropriate
- use touch-friendly interaction on mobile
- maintain clear hierarchy
- reveal additional information progressively

Cards should support the cinematic visual language of the site.

## Photography

Real photography should be prioritized over generic stock imagery whenever possible.

Photography should communicate:

- real trees
- forests
- workers
- professional equipment
- trucks
- chippers
- chainsaws
- Bobcats
- completed work
- before and after results

Images should feel authentic to the company and its service area.

Avoid artificial-looking imagery when real photography is available.

## Hero Visual Language

The Hero should establish the natural identity of the company.

The visual sequence should begin with a dense North American forest environment.

As the user scrolls:

- the camera moves deeper into the forest
- layers of trees create depth
- professional tree-service activity is gradually revealed
- equipment and workers appear naturally within the environment

The transition must feel like entering the operation rather than switching between unrelated sections.

## Navigation

The Navbar should remain minimal.

Desktop navigation should prioritize:

- logo
- primary navigation
- primary CTA

The navigation should not visually compete with the Hero.

Transparent navigation may be used over the Hero.

A solid or blurred background may appear when scrolling into content.

## Imagery and Overlays

When using full-screen imagery:

- preserve the visual subject
- avoid excessive darkening
- use gradients instead of heavy overlays when possible
- maintain cinematic depth

Do not apply the same overlay strength to every image.

## Responsive Design

The Design System must adapt to:

### Desktop

Use large cinematic compositions, generous spacing, hover states, and complex motion.

### Tablet

Reduce scale and spacing while preserving hierarchy and visual impact.

### Mobile

Prioritize:

- readability
- touch interaction
- performance
- clear hierarchy
- simplified motion

Hover-dependent interactions must have an equivalent touch interaction.

## Motion and Visual Effects

Motion should enhance the visual hierarchy.

Use:

- subtle scale
- opacity
- transform
- parallax
- reveal
- masking
- depth
- controlled transitions

Avoid excessive:

- bouncing
- rotating
- flashing
- unnecessary 3D effects

Animation should feel natural and cinematic.

## Consistency

All new UI must follow the established Design System.

Do not introduce:

- random colors
- random typography
- unrelated shadows
- inconsistent radii
- arbitrary animation styles
- unrelated visual patterns

When a new visual treatment is necessary, extend the existing system instead of creating an isolated style.

## Design Priority

When visual decisions conflict, prioritize in this order:

1. Usability
2. Readability
3. Conversion
4. Performance
5. Brand consistency
6. Visual effects

Visual complexity must never compromise usability or performance.
